# Workflow

```text
Grill (when the design branches) → Issue → branch → work → PR → human review → squash merge → retrospective
```

1. Grill when a requirement, the API, the wire format, or a roadmap boundary branches. Skill: `.agents/skills/grilling/SKILL.md`. Skip it when the Issue already has acceptance text and `docs/design.ja.md` settles the question. A question that rests on how a library behaves is asked after that behavior is measured. In V1c9 the Grill took it that Kestrel reports every failure to listen as `IOException`; Kestrel does so only for an address in use, and other socket errors, such as binding an address the machine does not have, stay `SocketException`, so the owner was asked again (U1, PR #233). A title or a term of the user documentation that a Grill decides is checked against the Japanese glossary of `.cursor/rules/user-docs.mdc` before it is recommended: V1c4f's Q4 gave the Japanese page the title 「…を有効化する」, while the glossary writes activation as アクティベーション; the translator found it, and the implementation changed it (PR #297). A question whose answer rests on a tool of this repository, such as the step runner, is asked after the tool's current rules are read: in V1c4d2 the Grill recommended page flows that met five rules of `eng/docs/Test-GuideSteps.ps1` (an `os` block takes no capture, a How-to page holds no `steps:prebuilt`, a background restarts the same program, one per page, and no shell), and the design review (R1 to R5, R7) changed them and put two of them (R2, R5) to the owner again (PR #281). A check that a Grill gives to a review is one the reviewing agent can do: an agent drives no graphical browser. In V1c4d2b the design review (R7) had the first-time reader try Chrome, Edge, and Firefox, which no agent could, and the page ended up saying it was not tried (PR #285, the owner's R1). A check that needs a person is asked of the owner, or the page says it was not tried.
2. One Issue is one PR. Create the Issue from a template in `.github/ISSUE_TEMPLATE/`. Blank issues are off.
3. A change to the meaning of the design is a `design` Issue that updates `docs/design.ja.md` before any implementation Issue (`.cursor/rules/design-docs.mdc`). The detail of a row that its own Grill decided may ship in that row's PR after an independent design review; that exception is in `design-docs.mdc`.
4. Acceptance text lives on the Issue. `docs/roadmap.md` keeps ID, title, Issue, and status only. Each sentence of Done when is checked against the behavior the Grill decided before it goes on the Issue: in V1c11 Done when said that the client fetches a new nonce after a bad header, while Q1 had decided that a bad header counts as none, so within the nonce's lifetime the client keeps the earlier one; the review (A2) found it, and the owner had it read as Q1 says (PR #245). A spike's result goes into the `## Result` section of the Issue body (`gh issue edit --body-file`) with the evidence and the reproduction commands, before the PR is called ready. The PR also sets its roadmap row to `done` and points **今** at the next row before it is called ready. A script that replaces a section of an Issue body finds the heading as a whole line (`^## Result$`), not as text, and compares the list of `## ` headings before and after the edit. In W0 the second update of the Result cut the body at the words "`## Result`" inside a Grill bullet and dropped the rest of the Grill and three sections; the design review found it, and the body came back from the edit history (PR #95, R1). A PR that fixes part of a later row changes that row's records in the same PR: the **フェーズ** line and the row in `docs/roadmap.md`, the row in the tables of design §20.7 and §22 with its list of points for the Grill, and a comment on that row's Issue that says what was fixed, by which test, and what is left. That Issue stays open. In V1d2 the fix for the correctness review's C1 also fixed the `elm-server` side of V1d3 (Issue #321), and the records still gave V1d3 both sides until the owner's review asked (PR #355, R1).
5. Branch from `origin/main` after a fetch: `type/<issue-number>-<slug>`, for example `feat/12-token-verify`. `type` is a Conventional Commits type.
6. Open the PR in the same turn that the branch is pushed. A PR opened before its verification and measurements are done, for a check that runs only on the runners (the PostgreSQL tests), is opened as a draft (`gh pr create --draft`) and marked ready when its body is complete. In M4b1 a PR opened that way read as ready to review while its body said the measurements were still running (PR #61). Use `.github/pull_request_template.md` (`.cursor/rules/pull-requests.mdc`). `Closes #N` stands alone on a line under `## Related` (`.cursor/rules/github-closing.mdc`). Before calling a PR ready, and before answering a review, read the checks of the pushed head (`gh pr checks <n>`): a local `./build.ps1` does not run on the runners. A PR that shows no checks is first checked for a conflict with `main` (`gh pr view <n> --json mergeable`): with a conflict GitHub makes no merge commit and starts no `pull_request` run. In PR #191 two pushes got no CI run, and the checks were run by `workflow_dispatch` before the conflict with the merged PR #189 was found. While checks run, read them yourself (`gh pr checks <n>`) instead of waiting for a notice, and go on as soon as they finish: in PR #191 the owner pointed out that CI had finished while the report still said it was running. `gh pr checks <n> --watch` started right after a push ends with exit code 1 when no check is registered yet, so a command that chains the gate, the push, and the watch reports a failure although the gate and the push passed: read the checks once before watching. It happened on the third push of PR #334. A CI log read with `gh run view --log-failed` holds its colors as text (`^[[31mfailed^[[m`), so a search for `failed <test name>` finds nothing: strip them first (`sed 's/\^\[\[[0-9;]*m//g'`). In PR #159 a count of past failures came out empty twice until they were stripped. `gh run view --job <id> --log` may name every step `UNKNOWN STEP`, so lines are picked by their content, not by the step's name: in PR #297 the log of PR #293's run, filtered by the step name, gave no line. A job's log can be read only after the whole run has ended (`gh run view --job <id> --log` says the run is still in progress), and a push to the same branch cancels the run that is in progress. When a value must come from a run's log, wait for that run to end before the next push: in PR #237 the time of Tutorial 4 came from the log of run 42362464212, and the fixes waited for it; the runs of 966a6bd and 9d8ee73 were cancelled by the next push. A run of the repository's default CodeQL setup cannot be re-run through the API: `gh run rerun` answers "This workflow run cannot be retried". A new run comes with the next push (the re-run button on the run's GitHub page was not tried); until then, the PR body says which check failed and why. In PR #253 `Analyze (javascript-typescript)` failed on a 504 while it downloaded the CodeQL bundle. `gh run rerun <id> --failed` runs again only the jobs that failed. When the `Report` job of `Measure` fails on a target, the measuring jobs passed, so it runs `Report` alone over the same result files and fails the same way: measure again with `gh run rerun <id>`. In PR #355 run 43150974969 was rerun once with `--failed` for nothing. When the AlmaLinux builds of `release.yml` (`Build linux-x64`, `Build linux-arm64`) stop with "The .NET SDK is X, not DOTNET_SDK_VERSION Y", the runner image has brought a newer SDK patch: the container uses the runner's SDK folder, and `global.json` rolls forward to the newest patch there. Compare the image version (`Runner Image`, `Version:`) and the `setup-dotnet` line "already installed" of a job that passed and one that failed. The fix raises `DOTNET_SDK_VERSION` in `release.yml` and `measure.yml`, the tag and digest of `ALPINE_SDK_IMAGE` (the registry's `Docker-Content-Digest` of the tag), and the image's row in design §21. Images roll out over days, so on the same day a run on the old image still passes. In PR #362 two Linux builds failed this way on image 20261111.370.1 while PR #360 had passed on 20261101.360.1 (PR #365).
7. A human reviews and squash-merges. An agent merges only when the owner names the PR and orders it; it squash-merges after the checks pass, with one message in the form of `.cursor/rules/conventional-commits.mdc`.
8. After the merge, delete the local and remote branch.
9. Retrospective, below, before the next feature branch.

## Issue templates and labels

Labels have the form `<axis>:<value>`. The only axis today is `type`.

| Template   | Label           | Commit / PR type           |
| ---------- | --------------- | -------------------------- |
| `feat`     | `type:feat`     | `feat`                     |
| `spike`    | `type:spike`    | `chore` (code is scratch)  |
| `bug`      | `type:bug`      | `fix`                      |
| `refactor` | `type:refactor` | `refactor`                 |
| `test`     | `type:test`     | `test`                     |
| `design`   | `type:docs`     | `docs`                     |
| `task`     | `type:chore`    | `chore` / `build` / `ci`   |

## Gates

- `./build.ps1`: tool restore, CSharpier check, restore, then Release and Debug builds with warnings as errors, each followed by every `tests/**/*.Tests.csproj`.
- The NativeAOT publish check runs where the Issue names it (`.cursor/rules/aot.mdc`).
- After each merge, read `main`'s CI. A red `main` gets its own Issue and PR before the next merge.
- A `main` run cancelled before any job started is not a failure. It was waiting in its concurrency group, and the next merge's run took its place: "By default, any existing `pending` job or workflow in the same concurrency group will be canceled" (GitHub Docs, Workflow syntax, `concurrency`). The newer run holds the cancelled commit, so read that run instead. After PR #164, the run of PR #167's merge was cancelled this way by the run of PR #169.

## Forbidden

- Starting implementation without an Issue number and a branch.
- Treating a roadmap title, or a file under `.dev/`, as permission to code.
- Copying acceptance text onto the roadmap.
- Adding or removing roadmap rows, or touching **Intentionally out of scope**, without a Grill. A row that the work shows to be needed is put to the owner as a question with a recommendation before the PR.
- Merging. Closing an Issue or PR without an order that names it.
- Ending a turn on a branch that has no Issue and no PR.

## Numbers

Create an Issue before its number is written anywhere: a branch name, a commit, a PR body, an ADR. Issue and PR numbers share one sequence; the next one is not predictable.

```text
# BAD — write `Closes #8` into the body, then create the Issue, which gets #9
# GOOD — create the Issue, read its number from the output, then write the body
```

Creating the Issue and using its number are separate commands. A chain such as `gh issue create ... && git switch -c docs/3-slug` writes a number before the output that gives it has been read. In D0 the agent chained the two and named the branch `docs/3-...` before reading the URL; the number happened to match (PR #4's retrospective).

## Stacked PRs

A second Issue that builds on an open PR gets its own branch and PR based on the first. A stacked PR is merged only after its base has merged. When the base merges, retarget the next PR to `main` first, then delete the merged branch.

## Retrospective

After a squash merge, in the turn that learns of it, write three points as a comment on the merged PR. Write "none" when a point is empty.

1. A lesson for `.cursor/rules/`, a skill, `docs/design.ja.md`, or one ADR under `docs/adr/`. Tacit knowledge the work relied on is a lesson: a way of measuring, a way a tool fails on this setup, a mistake the review or the owner had to catch.
2. The Issue that will carry it, if any.
3. Whether to refactor. Yes opens a `refactor` Issue that comes next. Postponing it needs a roadmap row (`.cursor/rules/defer.mdc`).

Then, without waiting for an ask, file a `chore` Issue and one PR that writes each lesson into the file it names. The next feature branch waits until that PR is open.

When a lesson PR merges while another PR is open, check the open PR against the new rule before calling it ready. In M2 the rule for testing readers of untrusted input (PR #10) merged after PR #12 opened, and the review, not the agent, found the readers PR #12 had added without those tests.

```text
# BAD — the PR merged; the next row starts; what the row taught stays in the conversation
# GOOD — the PR merged; three points on it; a chore PR with the lessons is open; then the next row
```
